sub: run the subtitle render outside osd->lock and dec_sub->lock

osd_render held osd->lock, and sub_get_bitmaps held dec_sub->lock, across
the decoder's whole render. The playloop takes both every iteration:
osd_set_text through update_osd_msg, and sub_read_packets plus two
sub_control calls through update_subtitle - which write_video also gates
queuing a frame on. On heavy typesetting a libass render is hundreds of
milliseconds to seconds on TV hardware, so for that long no audio was
filled and no video queued: AudioTrack underran and every frame due in
the window was dropped (edde746/plezy#2242, Fire TV Stick 4K Max Gen 2:
3.0 s of video took 3.8-4.0 s with 3-5 underruns per sign).

Release both locks around the render. dec_sub marks the decoder as
rendering; while it is, sub_read_packets keeps draining the demuxer but
queues packets (copies - cached_pkts may free the originals) instead of
decoding, defers segment switches, and never blocks; the two per-frame
controls (video params, animated check) are plain field writes and are
stashed. Everything else that touches sd (reset, select, redecode,
preload, get_text, get_times, other controls, a second get_bitmaps)
waits on a condition, and the render applies the queue in order when it
returns. osd marks the subtitle object as busy while its dec_sub is
rendering with osd->lock released; osd_set_sub (the caller destroys the
old dec_sub right after) and osd_free wait for it, and a second renderer
of a subtitle object waits too, keeping today's serialization.

Same device, same sign at 1080p: 3.0 s of video in 3.0 s, 0 underruns,
0 VO drops; a seek or stop that lands inside a render waits for it once
(1-2 s worst case), as before. ThreadSanitizer on a host build driving
random seeks, track switches, visibility, overrides, sub-reload and
loadfile-replace against Ex1/Ex2 with the render on the VO thread
reports nothing. The sign is still late by the render time; that is
libass cost, and no longer the player's.

